<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Aussa Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1734 CE – 1936 CE (approx.) · capital Aussa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The leading Afar state, centred on the Aussa oasis of the lower Awash, which defeated an Egyptian expedition and kept autonomy into the 20th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the collapse of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/imamate-of-aussa">Imamate of Aussa</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudaito-dynasty">Mudaito</a> clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar">Afar</a> took power in the oasis around 1734 under Kedafu, founder of a new dynasty. The sultanate controlled the irrigated lands of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awash-river">Awash</a> river, an island of farming in the deserts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar-triangle">Afar Triangle</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The sultan ruled with clan leaders over pastoral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar">Afar</a> and the farming population of the oasis, applying Islamic law alongside Afar customary law. The sultanate controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salt-trade">salt trade</a> between the coast and the highlands. In 1875 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Mahammad Hanfadhe destroyed an Egyptian expedition led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/werner-munzinger">Werner Munzinger</a>, securing its independence.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aussa">Aussa</a> accepted the overlordship of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a> but kept wide autonomy. Its sultan sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> during the 1936 invasion, and the sultanate was later reabsorbed into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federal-democratic-republic-of-ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. The dynasty continued under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alimirah-hanfare">Alimirah Hanfare</a>, a major figure in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar">Afar</a> politics until his death in 2011.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/aussa-sultanate.jpg" alt="Aussa Sultanate" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Sémhur · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aussa-modern_(Africa_orthographic_projection).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1734 CE – 1936 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Aussa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kedafu, Mahammad Hanfadhe, Alimirah Hanfare</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Afar</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Ethiopia, Eritrea, Djibouti</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sultanate of Aussa, Awsa</dd></dl></aside></div>
